WEBVTT

0
00:00:00.009 --> 00:00:00.970
Unschooling,

1
00:00:01.129 --> 00:00:05.250
a term first used by educator John Holt in the late 1970s,

2
00:00:05.469 --> 00:00:10.149
is a learner-centered approach built on trust in a child's natural curiosity.

3
00:00:10.489 --> 00:00:12.970
Often called self-directed education,

4
00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.409
it moves away from set grade levels.

5
00:00:15.555 --> 00:00:18.235
Standardized curricula and rigid schedules.

6
00:00:18.594 --> 00:00:19.274
Instead,

7
00:00:19.594 --> 00:00:22.694
learning happens organically through real-world immersion,

8
00:00:22.954 --> 00:00:24.024
personal projects,

9
00:00:24.274 --> 00:00:24.694
play,

10
00:00:24.834 --> 00:00:25.954
and daily life,

11
00:00:26.274 --> 00:00:29.104
allowing the child's natural interests to drive what,

12
00:00:29.354 --> 00:00:31.135
when and how they learn.

13
00:00:31.514 --> 00:00:32.514
In this environment,

14
00:00:32.755 --> 00:00:37.235
the parent shifts from a traditional instructor to a facilitator and co-learner,

15
00:00:37.514 --> 00:00:39.104
providing access to tools,

16
00:00:39.544 --> 00:00:40.104
resources,

17
00:00:40.235 --> 00:00:40.985
mentors,

18
00:00:41.194 --> 00:00:44.205
and experiences that fuel the child's passions.

19
00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.869
Whether a student spends weeks diving into marine biology,

20
00:00:48.090 --> 00:00:48.950
building a computer,

21
00:00:49.290 --> 00:00:50.790
or running a small business,

22
00:00:51.049 --> 00:00:54.919
unschooling views all of life as a rich educational environment,

23
00:00:55.209 --> 00:00:58.849
prioritizing intrinsic motivation and lifelong autonomy.